---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const sections = [
  {
    href: '/tools/',
    title: 'Tools',
    description: 'Browser-based CAD viewers, converters, and geometry utilities.'
  },
  {
    href: '/calculators/',
    title: 'Calculators',
    description: 'Gear ratio, beam deflection, bolt torque, and more engineering calculators.'
  },
  {
    href: '/reference/',
    title: 'Reference',
    description: 'Material properties, fastener sizes, thread specs, and other lookup tables.'
  },
  {
    href: '/guides/',
    title: 'Guides',
    description: 'Long-form guides on mechanical design, tolerancing, and using these tools.'
  },
  {
    href: '/services/',
    title: 'Services',
    description: 'Freelance mechanical engineering and CAD work, scoped and priced.'
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Free Browser CAD Tools & Calculators"
  description="Free browser-based mechanical engineering tools: CAD viewers, calculators, reference data, and design guides. Files never leave your computer."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <h1>Free browser CAD tools that never upload your files</h1>
    <p>
      Every tool on this site runs entirely in your browser using WebAssembly —
      nothing you open or convert is ever sent to a server. That makes it safe
      to use on proprietary or NDA-restricted geometry, and it's a large part
      of why we can offer these tools for free.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="hub-grid">
      {sections.map((s) => (
        <a class="hub-card" href={s.href}>
          <h3>{s.title}</h3>
          <p>{s.description}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Why everything runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      Most online CAD tools work by uploading your file to a server, converting
      it, and sending back a result — which means your geometry sits on someone
      else's machine, even if only briefly. Creek Mechanical's viewer and
      converters use a CAD kernel compiled to WebAssembly, so STEP, IGES, and
      BREP files are parsed and rendered directly on your device. Nothing you
      open here is ever transmitted anywhere, which matters if you're working
      with proprietary designs, client geometry under NDA, or anything else
      you'd rather not hand to a third-party server.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/calculators/">calculators</a> and <a href="/reference/">reference tables</a> cover
      the everyday lookups mechanical and design engineers reach for — bolt
      torque, bearing life, thread specs, tolerance stack-ups — while the
      <a href="/guides/">guides</a> go deeper into the reasoning behind them,
      like when to use worst-case versus RSS tolerancing, or how to read a
      GD&T feature control frame.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
